  Do not infer approval merely from Status: Accepted.

  Active-set gate

  Enforce exact set equality:

  ACTIVE.md
  = accepted current lineage tips
  = records available to Planit binding

  Fail if:

  • an active record is not accepted;
  • an accepted current record is absent from the active set;
  • an active record has no file;
  • a superseded record remains active;
  • the active set changes after the plan snapshot.

  Binding gate

  For every active record:

  • identify its condition;
  • identify its obligation;
  • identify its runnable binary gate;
  • register that gate in the architecture-decision enforcement table;
  • prove the gate runs in CI;
  • prove at least one negative fixture fails.

  The entire compile fails if any applicable decision is:

  • unbound;
  • bound only to prose;
  • bound to a missing gate;
  • marked as an enforcement gap.

  That means the current gap rows cannot coexist with a claim that all active decisions are binding.

  3. Enforce BBP/BBA across all code

  First define the closed-world file classes. Every executable artifact must be exactly one of:

  1. Noun implementation
    • owns adjectives;
    • exposes contracted verbs;
    • mutates its own state only;
    • includes verb schemas and noun tests.

  2. Goal
    • orchestrates noun verbs and external effects;
    • owns no noun state;
    • performs no private noun mutation.

  3. Primitive adapter
    • performs one approved external operation;
    • contains no domain or process policy.

  4. Thin entrypoint
    • parses input;
    • calls one noun verb or goal;
    • translates output and failure;
    • contains no state machine or policy.

  This classification must cover Python, shell, workflow files, and generated code. Unclassified executable
  files fail.

  Consequence for release scripts

  scripts/nlc-release.sh may remain only as a thin entrypoint. Its current state machine, identity selection,
  remediation policy, tag policy, and transition logic must move into canonical noun/goal packages. The
  shell may invoke the selected action; it may not select another action.

  Repository-wide gates

  Use a complete executable-file inventory—not an allowlist of selected emit modules.

  For every executable file, require:

  • classification;
  • canonical package owner;
  • declared boundary input, output, and failure mode;
  • applicable architecture-decision bindings;
  • tests for its adjectives or orchestration;
  • provenance in an emit manifest;
  • no uncovered executable statements.

  Add two gates:

  • Change gate: every changed executable line is covered before commit.
  • Repository gate: every executable file is covered before verification or release.

  The repository gate must report:

  total executable files
  = noun implementations
  + goals
  + primitive adapters
  + thin entrypoints

  Any remainder greater than zero fails.

